<div class="component container col-12">
    <div class="component-content" >

    <div class="component footnotes col-12">
        <div class="component-content">
<div class="cf-footnotes">
    <h5>Footnotes</h5>
        <ol>
                    <li id="cf-fn-1">Before the rational expectations revolution, policy rules were advocated by monetarists who thought that central bank discretion should be curtailed because discretionary monetary policy was the primary source of macroeconomic fluctuations.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-1" aria-label="Return from footnote 1.">Return to 1</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-2">Kydland, Finn E. and Edward C. Prescott. 1977. "Rules Rather than Discretion; The Inconsistency of Optimal Plans," <em>Journal of Political Economy</em>, 85, 3 (June), 473-92.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-2" aria-label="Return from footnote 2.">Return to 2</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-3">Athanasios Orphanides. 2002. <a rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" class="offsite" href="http://www.federalreserve.gov/pubs/feds/2002/200208/200208pap.pdf">Monetary Policy Rules and the Great Inflation,&rdquo;</a> Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (January).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-3" aria-label="Return from footnote 3.">Return to 3</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-4">John Taylor (2000) provides a nice summary of this progression in economic thought. See &ldquo;How the Rational Expectations Revolution Has Changed Macroeconomic Policy Research"; Revised Draft: February 29, 2000; lecture presented at the 12th World Congress of the International Economic Association, Buenos Aires, Argentina, August 24, 1999.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-4" aria-label="Return from footnote 4.">Return to 4</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-5">See for example, Athanasios Orphanides, &ldquo;Monetary Policy in Deflation: The Liquidity Trap in History and Practice.&rdquo; The North American Journal of Economics and Finance, 15(1), 101-124, (2004).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-5" aria-label="Return from footnote 5.">Return to 5</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-6">Ben S. Bernanke, &ldquo;Economic Policy: Lessons from History.&rdquo;&nbsp; 43rd Annual Alexander Hamilton Awards Dinner, Center for the Study of the Presidency and Congress, Washington, DC.&nbsp; April 8, 2010.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-6" aria-label="Return from footnote 6.">Return to 6</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-7">See Statements <a rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" class="offsite" href="http://www.boj.or.jp/en/announcements/release_2012/k121030a.pdf">&ldquo;Enhancement of Monetary Easing,&rdquo;</a> and <a rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" class="offsite" href="http://www.boj.or.jp/en/announcements/release_2012/k121030b.pdf">&ldquo;Measures Aimed at Overcoming Deflation,&rdquo;</a> Bank of Japan, October 30, 2012.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-7" aria-label="Return from footnote 7.">Return to 7</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-8">See Owen Humpage, &ldquo;<a href="/publications/economic-commentary/2012/ec-201209-communication-credibility-and-price-stability-lessons-learned-from-japan">Communication, Credibility, and Price Stability: Lessons Learned from Japan</a>," Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland Economic Commentary 2012-09, July 2, 2012.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-8" aria-label="Return from footnote 8.">Return to 8</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-9">See the speech by Federal Reserve Vice Chair Janet L. Yellen, &ldquo;Unconventional Monetary Policy and Central Bank Communications,&rdquo; February 25, 2011, at the University of Chicago Booth School of Business U.S. Monetary Policy Forum, New York, New York.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-9" aria-label="Return from footnote 9.">Return to 9</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-10">See Chairman Ben S. Bernanke, <a rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" class="offsite" href="http://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/testimony/bernanke20100210a.htm">&ldquo;Federal Reserve's exit strategy, Before the Committee on Financial Services, U.S. House of Representatives, Washington, D.C.&ldquo;</a>; February 10, 2010, and <a rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" class="offsite" href="http://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomcminutes20110622.htm">Minutes of the FOMC</a>, June 21-22, 2011
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-10" aria-label="Return from footnote 10.">Return to 10</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-11">For illustrative projections of the balance sheet guided by the exit principles, see Seth B. Carpenter, Jane E. Ihrig, Elizabeth C. Klee, Alexander H. Boote, and Daniel W. Quinn (2012), &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" class="offsite" href="http://www.federalreserve.gov/pubs/feds/2012/201256/201256pap.pdf">The Federal Reserve's Balance Sheet: A Primer and Projections,&rdquo;&nbsp;</a>Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (November).
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-11" aria-label="Return from footnote 11.">Return to 11</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-12">See the speech by Federal Reserve Vice Chair Janet L. Yellen,&nbsp;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" class="offsite" href="http://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/yellen20120606a.htm">&ldquo;Perspectives on Monetary Policy,&rdquo;</a> June 6, 2012, At the Boston Economic Club Dinner, Boston, Massachusetts, especially footnotes 10 and 11
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-12" aria-label="Return from footnote 12.">Return to 12</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-13"><a rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" class="offsite" href="http://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/press/monetary/20120125c.htm">The FOMC announced</a> numerical objectives for price stability and maximum employment on January 25, 2012 to further increase its transparency, credibility, and accountability.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-13" aria-label="Return from footnote 13.">Return to 13</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-14">Chung, Hess, Jean-Philippe Laforte, David Reifschneider, and John C. Williams. 2012. <a rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" class="offsite" href="http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1538-4616.2011.00478.x/abstract">&ldquo;Have We Underestimated the Likelihood and Severity of Zero Lower Bound Events?&rdquo;</a> <em>Journal of Money, Credit, and Banking&nbsp;</em>44(s1), pp. 47&ndash;82.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-14" aria-label="Return from footnote 14.">Return to 14</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-15">For his review of the literature and its implications for monetary policy, see Michael Woodford, <a rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" class="offsite" href="http://kansascityfed.org/publicat/sympos/2012/mw.pdf">&ldquo;Methods of Policy Accommodation at the Interest-Rate Lower Bound,&rdquo;</a> Columbia University, August 20, 2012.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-15" aria-label="Return from footnote 15.">Return to 15</a>
                    </li>
        </ol>
